C/README.md:1: # jsonpatch
C/README.md:2: 
C/README.md:3: A JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) and JSON Patch (RFC 6902) library and command-line tool.
C/README.md:4: Node.js built-ins only, no dependencies.
C/README.md:5: 
C/README.md:6: ## Library
C/README.md:7: 
C/README.md:8: ```js
C/README.md:9: import { parsePointer, applyPatch, PatchError } from './jsonpatch.mjs';
C/README.md:10: 
C/README.md:11: parsePointer('/a~1b/~01'); // ['a/b', '~1']
C/README.md:12: 
C/README.md:13: const result = applyPatch({ b: 0 }, [{ op: 'add', path: '/1', value: 5 }]);
C/README.md:14: // { '1': 5, b: 0 }
C/README.md:15: 
C/README.md:16: try {
C/README.md:17:   applyPatch({}, [{ op: 'test', path: '/x', value: 1 }]);
C/README.md:18: } catch (err) {
C/README.md:19:   if (err instanceof PatchError) console.log(err.message, err.index);
C/README.md:20:   // "operation 0: path not found" 0
C/README.md:21: }
C/README.md:22: ```
C/README.md:23: 
C/README.md:24: - `parsePointer(pointer)` returns the decoded tokens. It throws `TypeError('pointer must be a string')`
C/README.md:25:   for a non-string and `SyntaxError('invalid pointer')` for an invalid pointer.
C/README.md:26: - `applyPatch(doc, patch)` returns a new document; `doc` and `patch` are never changed and the
C/README.md:27:   result shares no objects with them. Numbers (including `-0`) and member order are preserved.
C/README.md:28: - Every operation is checked for form first; then they are applied in order. Errors are
C/README.md:29:   `PatchError`s with `message` `operation <i>: <reason>` and `index` `<i>`, or
C/README.md:30:   `patch must be an array` with `index` `null`.
C/README.md:31: 
C/README.md:32: Form reasons: `not an object`, `bad op`, `bad path`, `bad from`, `missing value`,
C/README.md:33: `cannot remove root`, `cannot move into own child`.
C/README.md:34: Apply reasons: `path not found`, `from not found`, `bad index`, `test failed`.
C/README.md:35: 
C/README.md:36: ## Command line
C/README.md:37: 
C/README.md:38: ```sh
C/README.md:39: node jsonpatch-cli.mjs <patch> [<doc>]
C/README.md:40: ```
C/README.md:41: 
C/README.md:42: Reads the patch from `<patch>` and the document from `<doc>`; a missing `<doc>` or an argument
C/README.md:43: of `-` means standard input (only one input may be stdin). Prints the result as
C/README.md:44: `JSON.stringify(result, null, 2)` plus a newline.
C/README.md:45: 
C/README.md:46: ```sh
C/README.md:47: echo '{"b":0}' | node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json
C/README.md:48: ```
C/README.md:49: 
C/README.md:50: Inputs are UTF-8 (a leading byte order mark is ignored), at most 1048576 bytes and at most
C/README.md:51: 1000 levels deep.
C/README.md:52: 
C/README.md:53: | Exit | Meaning | stderr |
C/README.md:54: | ---- | ------- | ------ |
C/README.md:55: | 0 | success | (nothing) |
C/README.md:56: | 1 | read error | `Error: <name>: no such file` / `is a directory` / `too large` / `cannot read` |
C/README.md:57: | 2 | bad arguments | `Usage: node jsonpatch-cli.mjs <patch> [<doc>]` |
C/README.md:58: | 3 | bad input | `Error: <name>: invalid UTF-8` / `invalid JSON` / `too deep` |
C/README.md:59: | 4 | patch failed | `Error: <PatchError message>` |
C/README.md:60: 
C/README.md:61: `<name>` is the argument as given, or `stdin`.
C/README.md:62: 
C/README.md:63: ## Tests
C/README.md:64: 
C/README.md:65: ```sh
C/README.md:66: npm test
C/README.md:67: ```

