B/README.md:1: # jsonpatch
B/README.md:2: 
B/README.md:3: A dependency-free JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) and JSON Patch (RFC 6902) library and
B/README.md:4: command-line tool for Node.js.
B/README.md:5: 
B/README.md:6: ## Library
B/README.md:7: 
B/README.md:8: ```js
B/README.md:9: import { parsePointer, applyPatch, PatchError } from './jsonpatch.mjs';
B/README.md:10: 
B/README.md:11: parsePointer('/a~1b/~0c/0'); // ['a/b', '~c', '0']
B/README.md:12: 
B/README.md:13: const doc = { a: [1, 2] };
B/README.md:14: const result = applyPatch(doc, [
B/README.md:15:   { op: 'add', path: '/a/1', value: 9 },
B/README.md:16:   { op: 'test', path: '/a', value: [1, 9, 2] },
B/README.md:17: ]);
B/README.md:18: // result: { a: [1, 9, 2] }; doc is unchanged
B/README.md:19: 
B/README.md:20: try {
B/README.md:21:   applyPatch(doc, [{ op: 'remove', path: '/nope' }]);
B/README.md:22: } catch (e) {
B/README.md:23:   e instanceof PatchError; // true
B/README.md:24:   e.message; // 'operation 0: path not found'
B/README.md:25:   e.index; // 0
B/README.md:26: }
B/README.md:27: ```
B/README.md:28: 
B/README.md:29: - `parsePointer(pointer)` returns the decoded tokens. It throws a `TypeError`
B/README.md:30:   (`pointer must be a string`) for non-strings and a `SyntaxError`
B/README.md:31:   (`invalid pointer`) for malformed pointers.
B/README.md:32: - `applyPatch(doc, patch)` returns a new document; neither `doc` nor `patch` is
B/README.md:33:   modified and the result shares no objects with them. All operations are
B/README.md:34:   validated before any is applied. Failures throw a `PatchError` whose `message`
B/README.md:35:   is `operation <i>: <reason>` and whose `index` is `<i>` (or
B/README.md:36:   `patch must be an array` with `index` null).
B/README.md:37: 
B/README.md:38: Reasons: `not an object`, `bad op`, `bad path`, `bad from`, `missing value`,
B/README.md:39: `cannot remove root`, `cannot move into own child`, `path not found`,
B/README.md:40: `from not found`, `bad index`, `test failed`.
B/README.md:41: 
B/README.md:42: ## Command line
B/README.md:43: 
B/README.md:44: ```sh
B/README.md:45: node jsonpatch-cli.mjs <patch> [<doc>]
B/README.md:46: ```
B/README.md:47: 
B/README.md:48: Reads the patch from the file `<patch>` and the document from `<doc>`. A missing
B/README.md:49: `<doc>`, or `-`, means standard input (only one input may come from stdin). On
B/README.md:50: success the patched document is printed as JSON indented by two spaces.
B/README.md:51: 
B/README.md:52: ```sh
B/README.md:53: echo '{"a":1}' | node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json
B/README.md:54: node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json doc.json
B/README.md:55: ```
B/README.md:56: 
B/README.md:57: Exit codes:
B/README.md:58: 
B/README.md:59: | Code | Meaning |
B/README.md:60: | ---- | ------- |
B/README.md:61: | 0 | success |
B/README.md:62: | 2 | usage error |
B/README.md:63: | 1 | read error (`no such file`, `is a directory`, `too large` (> 1 MiB), `cannot read`) |
B/README.md:64: | 3 | `invalid UTF-8`, `invalid JSON`, `too deep` (> 1000 levels) |
B/README.md:65: | 4 | the patch could not be applied |
B/README.md:66: 
B/README.md:67: Errors are printed as a single line on stderr, e.g. `Error: doc.json: invalid JSON`
B/README.md:68: or `Error: operation 2: test failed`.
B/README.md:69: 
B/README.md:70: ## Tests
B/README.md:71: 
B/README.md:72: ```sh
B/README.md:73: npm test
B/README.md:74: ```
D/README.md:1: # jsonpatch
D/README.md:2: 
D/README.md:3: A JSON Pointer (RFC 6901) and JSON Patch (RFC 6902) library and command-line tool for Node.js. It has no dependencies.
D/README.md:4: 
D/README.md:5: ## Library
D/README.md:6: 
D/README.md:7: ```js
D/README.md:8: import { parsePointer, applyPatch, PatchError } from './jsonpatch.mjs';
D/README.md:9: 
D/README.md:10: parsePointer('/a~1b/~01'); // ['a/b', '~1']
D/README.md:11: 
D/README.md:12: const doc = { b: 0, list: [1, 2] };
D/README.md:13: const result = applyPatch(doc, [
D/README.md:14:   { op: 'add', path: '/1', value: 5 },
D/README.md:15:   { op: 'move', from: '/list/0', path: '/list/-' },
D/README.md:16:   { op: 'test', path: '/list', value: [2, 1] },
D/README.md:17: ]);
D/README.md:18: // result: { '1': 5, b: 0, list: [2, 1] }. doc is unchanged.
D/README.md:19: 
D/README.md:20: try {
D/README.md:21:   applyPatch(doc, [{ op: 'remove', path: '/missing' }]);
D/README.md:22: } catch (err) {
D/README.md:23:   err instanceof PatchError; // true
D/README.md:24:   err.message;               // 'operation 0: path not found'
D/README.md:25:   err.index;                 // 0
D/README.md:26: }
D/README.md:27: ```
D/README.md:28: 
D/README.md:29: - `parsePointer(pointer)` returns the decoded tokens. It throws a `TypeError` (`pointer must be a string`) when the pointer is not a string, and a `SyntaxError` (`invalid pointer`) when the pointer is invalid.
D/README.md:30: - `applyPatch(doc, patch)` returns a new document. It never changes `doc` or `patch`, and the result shares no objects or arrays with them. All operations are checked before any of them is applied. The result keeps `-0` and member order.
D/README.md:31: - `PatchError` has an `index` property. It is `null` when the patch is not an array (message `patch must be an array`). Otherwise it is the position of the failing operation, and the message is `operation <i>: <reason>`.
D/README.md:32: 
D/README.md:33: The reasons are: `not an object`, `bad op`, `bad path`, `bad from`, `missing value`, `cannot remove root`, `cannot move into own child`, `path not found`, `from not found`, `bad index` and `test failed`.
D/README.md:34: 
D/README.md:35: ## Command line
D/README.md:36: 
D/README.md:37: ```sh
D/README.md:38: node jsonpatch-cli.mjs <patch> [<doc>]
D/README.md:39: ```
D/README.md:40: 
D/README.md:41: The tool reads the patch from the file `<patch>` and the document from `<doc>`. If `<doc>` is missing, or if an argument is `-`, that input comes from standard input. On success it prints the result as 2-space-indented JSON and exits with 0.
D/README.md:42: 
D/README.md:43: ```sh
D/README.md:44: echo '{"a":1}' | node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json
D/README.md:45: node jsonpatch-cli.mjs patch.json doc.json > out.json
D/README.md:46: ```
D/README.md:47: 
D/README.md:48: Each input must be UTF-8 (a leading byte order mark is ignored), at most 1 MiB, and nested at most 1000 levels deep. On an error the tool prints one line to stderr and exits with one of these codes:
D/README.md:49: 
D/README.md:50: | Exit | Cause | Example message |
D/README.md:51: | --- | --- | --- |
D/README.md:52: | 2 | wrong arguments, or both inputs on stdin | `Usage: node jsonpatch-cli.mjs <patch> [<doc>]` |
D/README.md:53: | 1 | read error | `Error: doc.json: no such file` / `is a directory` / `too large` / `cannot read` |
D/README.md:54: | 3 | bad content | `Error: doc.json: invalid UTF-8` / `invalid JSON` / `too deep` |
D/README.md:55: | 4 | the patch failed | `Error: operation 0: bad op` |
D/README.md:56: 
D/README.md:57: ## Tests
D/README.md:58: 
D/README.md:59: ```sh
D/README.md:60: npm test
D/README.md:61: ```

